#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128862 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0a99e46a-2104-5285-862b-96b98e7a2f85" :question/math-academy-id "e-12482" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cfd94391c0090bc628e4203af3ff15ff7407c9ecc4cc4e7a6f6ac7a1cf36b25.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass ![](images/a7/a76c5969032432301de3fc7cda0a904f10aadae5b58e8974db1f29ee18ec14c0.png) occupies the region ![](images/3c/3c51a5e31ce9f37895e4e38935b015ba8fd40373baa1c8735bd63f3592968af9.png) in the first quadrant enclosed by the lines ![](images/29/2997a64fa661559850be321f40a59dd8b408fe6cb662615d51b53cff07ac5a89.png) and ![](images/eb/eb52b396a667b392d8cd2c0913065ec8c9014c014f9284bff1adb3eced294d0c.png) and the ![](images/4d/4d1c3fb0851b2e9ab223c4d204d3554f710a9db8f3442d5869917828d94fe837.png)-axis. The mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the ![](images/76/76c6f393964ba1978d27eb9fd4cf2c604384750dba74b6c08e891587e2834a91.png)-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the ![](images/ea/eafdfda4fc9654c2c5aae57bb072fafa8636c8334ae4b4eed8ff5a7c6076ff4f.png)-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina ![](images/42/42913b72caaae9f0740c30760baff88881610a1ced3ff3859222d27d1b8656b4.png) with mass density function ![](images/3e/3ee7e6579dec5241a245eab4eaf1b1fa3c180e6ec75a557baa9b2497b55558e2.png) we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/84/84b2f388da9d980669e8b0d6370edb0a12ef82752f16b4fb1b6dc6ee4c44c03d.png)-axis is given by\n![](images/60/60a59f8ad0379b033d9d2bac9d67afe5c40a43f6d1f2bb6169b90ca1a78221aa.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/00/00ad0bc1dadfb43a63229d80b11e02988bbe482c5d5122ff28c54bc3d2309231.png)-axis is given by\n![](images/b3/b36595e561b6dadab98447cbca0621828e651a2a3616504357aa90d13a7a20b2.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n![](images/4c/4cfac7a7f94c2c55d261fd1e84810bf193920e1003cc0fdc6e841fcb8d8b3f1d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/05/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) is a type I region with representation\n![](images/e4/e4896170bd06ee1f66dd595e504209977df6611d8c5c4fa0cbf28d2dc146beaf.png)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1301c17b1dbb8b48716d09ad6f0a7a105917ea7a1c0e942bbe087b12772f254.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as \n![](images/3b/3bc1fabeed9c38d36d49380c17ba47c6c0a9d6a1723bed3a4f6e33467bd893e2.png)\nwhere ![](images/c2/c26b2d7388707b35315aac38bf33a36573a637e3410d1a8e2797fc4164f6d438.png) is a constant of proportionality. \n\nSince the density contains the unknown constant ![](images/d6/d61c1c56d7c970921acb23214ab2ece4bbd33fefbf212b481ca95fd65fb926df.png), we first use the total mass ![](images/86/86f38d0431d36a6b7010ff7611af19c2c54116e9465f15e5a36cf85c933e3054.png) to determine ![](images/90/9025c6a94271bd94be0bb930ab14cdb7611034b480b7b56ca76767f957f789c7.png)\n\nWe can find an expression for ![](images/e3/e375b825a2d61d973c964706a6bf27c9af8a19b3594f02cdf70afb62b988e66b.png) in terms of ![](images/6e/6e32bbbc4bffbd45f252c9185c7760901b027dd554ef49d044c78e87331029b4.png) by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n![](images/e4/e4fc94647cb930e64247e9bac85885f6ac4fae1ed132be30f35db0f3723f4f43.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/39/399765a98ec95ac5bc4deb478463b2787bfa1d6a17a2ea0c9d6f026dd6b79563.png) and hence ![](images/f1/f1e138092994505279c36db8afdf2e7df102c4efbdb5803515077b076009bf81.png) Therefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](images/00/00ad0bc1dadfb43a63229d80b11e02988bbe482c5d5122ff28c54bc3d2309231.png)-axis is\n![](images/3a/3a0e13d8b9068b72cc88b446cfa2fa0c3ac418b9001013a0f630cb5d621b3f6a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128863 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "dbf6a3b7-abb9-5f62-83af-00169c673bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12483" :question/problem "![](images/12/12fb5f07885b074f983cab3a5fba0faff607d8aa34d7cd3abe4e94abf53dac25.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r = 1$ and $r = 2$ and the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(r,\\theta),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_x = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3\\sin^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3\\cos^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{r^2 \\cos^2{\\theta}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129030 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/id #uuid "c317696a-899d-5af5-9bf4-2d38862f5877" :question/math-academy-id "e-8542" :question/problem "![](images/de/de882e240d0a6ca824e77397fe9c6b51eb94d36c2e5e3524422ef0a0f28103de.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass ![](images/48/48c72d7f57bb7f3163fa37903a032773c12ddce6fdc0e0bee8818259aba9e465.png) and uniform mass density occupies the region ![](images/05/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) enclosed between the ![](images/54/54d73239bada862f9b8d6d3d7a4f73bea15432668f6b2116098faa02a4b3d435.png)-axis and the lines ![](images/38/3879c9475dcb86235e269108d73767f2e631eee68e65aacbfffbc22ada2033e1.png) and ![](images/52/52ab6e6677e9404e8b08bfff7b90cbfb332b344d4fe8ecc19b603ef05616ebf7.png) as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the ![](images/f0/f0a3d06d2c55c6889a22a1cc4e11adb35b71e24e1395e6d6048c1135620c225f.png)-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina ![](images/28/28390f00cdb34a228868c36035ff66f1ea65bf7ff5e592fa473fca18d6addbcc.png) with mass density function ![](images/c9/c97ba2315cf57f0ca52513a553203387b121396b6b0a5c96fc9a67e0ae6798d4.png) we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/24/245bebfeb6cb95babe872c0185e33d598fb9dd08c8950252c07cdb5f5c8f87d4.png)-axis is given by\n![](images/b8/b8379c5ab1146e79464ee5677bc05edac3c6a9368629746b08c9a9e66109681c.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/4c/4cf2e90080f1ae459e72ac253d6617d0defdcc3be58a1ca385bddc3215034eca.png)-axis is given by\n![](images/ac/ac1949df7033d3a968286404f658fea6ad7de10f446d6ba8f4090bf40fa26afa.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n![](images/50/509782164a1c349c9ba8b6399b252decae9b022ffde15d840bc68b123872071d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/3c/3c51a5e31ce9f37895e4e38935b015ba8fd40373baa1c8735bd63f3592968af9.png) is a type I region with representation\n\n![](images/e6/e666ae60e603116812051833c70d32199e3f146e23fc99260c274fb546bfeef8.png)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/68/68904cbee1839a61f4d65c5ff03066d8f28b8a5e070f6ddb00c82c29cfd93fcd.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate ![](images/ee/eebd5724c98ea00681ef901dabae1d0b3428aff0c6e6a9cdadc020ed46f129c7.png) is \n![](images/02/0285f4ab3e7a9c974f244d0ab517ca737d2a5aec35bfbceb779037d54ce00f98.png)\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n![](images/d3/d3089c43ce61bf64a57a6d64b38987b91a6c8f9aa9f06d106f4c124ed430dd09.png)\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](images/23/235efd30dbd8ca8883230793c8b79882afd19443c612b54f30b5cd507a646c9c.png)-axis is\n\n![](images/14/148b58f76868ac2f7a85e68627f91f2fa47c6c6a2bd5bcb67af8ba8af011167a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bee275c6-cca6-515f-b737-597ecca278a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8543" :question/problem "![](images/46/46d6a57700d1df7f2de9a46507821a8553876eb956be6ef87a455d5ca0fabafb.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 4xy,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nI_x =  \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nI_y =  \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/88/8846da0fe3b1e8369a9277bbaba1e082d040136a71105a5816af7b13d2788027.png)\n\nUsing the formula for $I_x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}4x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{y}^{4}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x-{x}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{10}}{10}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0a8b33a-6039-541e-b58e-1f0a29efa688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126690} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc5d6a1-85df-58ec-8f12-88aeac9bf8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126691} {:answer/id #uuid "f26bff63-e8ea-527f-8e55-bced519e9384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126692} {:answer/id #uuid "18fe4b91-2465-5f90-aa80-4b1a75799c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126693} {:answer/id #uuid "27fbd22f-ff3d-5928-ab73-3c683a066225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126694}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dabbdf32-9b8d-5ae1-a71a-6f12fe91d019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126689}] :question/id #uuid "f0af98bb-12cf-5c0f-a924-a0fe039adf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-162906" :question/problem "![](images/64/64cadaa8b079c80659d661be55709e572c05b185a6b6fdb60e24801a9d1e84a5.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y = 2x$ and $y = 3 - x$, as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8147d1fc-c210-5b6e-8737-184cbc1